<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Glastonbury Abbey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Glastonbury Abbey was a Benedictine monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, founded by the seventh century and patronised by the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> kings. Reformed by Dunstan in the tenth century, it became one of the richest abbeys in England and was linked in legend with Joseph of Arimathea and King Arthur, whose supposed grave the monks found in 1191. It was dissolved in 1539.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glastonbury-abbey.jpg" alt="Glastonbury Abbey" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steve Slater · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glastonbury_Abbey_church_view_from_east.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
